@startuml D2_PartA_Tool_Design_Flow
' ============================================================
' DOMAIN 2 — PART A: TOOL DESIGN (Topics 1–11)
' FULL FLOW: from a request arriving to a correctly-routed,
' validly-formed, well-scoped tool call.
' CORE TRAP: the DESCRIPTION is the primary routing signal,
' NOT the name, schema, or output type.
' ============================================================
title Domain 2 · Part A — Tool Design (Topics 1–11)\n(Request -> correct ROUTING -> valid INPUTS -> scoped, controlled call)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF6CC

start

:User request arrives\ne.g. "find my March orders";

' ---------- TOPIC 1: HOW CLAUDE SELECTS TOOLS ----------
partition "T1 · How Claude SELECTS Tools" {
  :Claude reads EVERY tool DESCRIPTION in context;
  #LightCyan:Does SEMANTIC MATCHING\n(intent of request vs MEANING of each description);
  note right
    == THE ONE BIG IDEA ==
    DESCRIPTION = the PRIMARY ROUTING SIGNAL.
    * Name = a hint for HUMANS reading code
    * Schema / output type = matter, but do NOT route
    Re-evaluated FRESH every turn (prior call = irrelevant).
    Can't keyword-stuff: it matches MEANING, not word count.
    ----
    WHY USED : Claude has many tools; something must pick one.
    SOLVES   : the routing problem — right tool for this intent.
    EXAMPLE  : "find my March orders" -> matches get_order_history.
    WHO CALLS: the MODEL selects; your app only runs the winner.
  end note
}

if (Description makes ONE tool the obvious winner?) then (YES)
  #LightGreen:**CORRECT CALL** — right tool, immediately;
else (NO — weak/overlapping descriptions)
  #Pink:One of 3 BAD OUTCOMES;
  note right
    == 4 ROUTING OUTCOMES (name them) ==
    1. CORRECT CALL — precise description
    2. MISROUTE — wrong tool (overlap / no specificity)
    3. NO CALL — nothing matched the intent
    4. FALLBACK — Claude asks user to clarify
       (the SAFE failure — better than a confident wrong call)
    ----
    == 3 DESCRIPTION FAILURE MODES ==
    a. VAGUE INTENT — says WHAT, not WHEN to use
    b. MISSING SCOPE — never says what it WON'T handle
    c. NO OUTPUT SIGNAL — never says what it RETURNS
    "processes data" hits ALL THREE at once.
  end note
endif

' ---------- TOPIC 2: WRITING DESCRIPTIONS ----------
partition "T2 · Writing Descriptions that ROUTE" {
  :Write description with 3 REQUIRED components;
  note right
    1. WHAT the tool does
    2. WHEN to use it — and WHEN NOT (negative scoping)
    3. WHAT it returns
    ....
    WEAK:  "handles orders"
    STRONG:"Retrieves customer order history by
            customer ID and date range; returns a
            list of orders with status + timestamps.
            Use for purchase history, NOT billing."
    (Applies to CLIENT, SERVER, and MCP tools alike.)
    ----
    WHY USED : the description IS the routing signal (T1).
    SOLVES   : misroute / no-call from vague or overlapping text.
    EXAMPLE  : add "Use for purchase history, NOT billing" to
               separate get_orders from get_invoices.
    WHO CALLS: YOU (tool author) write it; the MODEL reads it.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 3–5: SCHEMA CORRECTNESS ----------
partition "T3–T5 · Getting the ARGUMENTS right" {
  :T3 · Design INPUT SCHEMA for precision\n(start_date : string YYYY-MM-DD, described);
  note right
    Description picks the RIGHT TOOL.
    Schema fills the ARGUMENTS correctly.
    Every ambiguity left in = a malformed call at runtime.
    ----
    WHY USED : routing picks the tool; the args still need shape.
    SOLVES   : malformed / ambiguous calls the backend rejects.
    EXAMPLE  : start_date : string, "ISO YYYY-MM-DD" (described).
    WHO CALLS: YOU define the schema; the MODEL fills the values.
  end note

  :T4 · REQUIRED vs OPTIONAL + ENUMS + CONSTRAINTS;
  note right
    * Mark REQUIRED only if tool can't run without it
      (extra required fields => Claude invents values)
    * ENUM: status in [pending, shipped, delivered]
      => Claude physically can't pass "in transit"
    * CONSTRAINTS: min/max on page_size
    Moves validation from your CODE into the SCHEMA.
    ----
    WHY USED : narrow what the model is allowed to produce.
    SOLVES   : invented values, out-of-range args, bad enums.
    EXAMPLE  : status enum [pending,shipped,delivered] => Claude
               physically cannot pass "in transit".
    WHO CALLS: YOU set required/enums/limits; MODEL must obey.
  end note

  #Yellow:T5 · STRICT TOOL USE;
  note right
    Strict mode CONSTRAINS GENERATION (like autocorrect),
    it does NOT reject after the fact (not spellcheck).
    => Claude can only PRODUCE a conforming call.
    If info EXISTS -> lands in right field, right type.
    If info is MISSING -> Claude ASKS or re-routes,
    it does NOT fire a call with the field blank.
    ----
    WHY USED : the MODEL generates args; they may not conform.
    SOLVES   : structurally invalid calls (bad type, missing req,
               unknown enum, extra param).
    EXAMPLE  : forces status into the enum set, not free text.
    WHO CALLS: a MODE you enable; it constrains the MODEL.
    KEY      : guarantees VALID, not RIGHT — model can still
               pick the wrong room. Schema+descriptions -> right.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 6–7: FAILURE HANDLING ----------
partition "T6–T7 · When the CALL comes back" {
  if (Tool errored?) then (YES)
    #LightBlue:T6 · Return a USEFUL error\n(what went wrong + what to do next);
    note right
      Errors are written for the MODEL, not a log file.
      BAD:  "error: request failed"
      GOOD: "No customer with that ID —
             verify ID or search by email."
      => gives Claude its next move.
      ----
      WHY USED : the error text is what Claude reasons on next.
      SOLVES   : blind retries / dead-ends from generic errors.
      EXAMPLE  : "room unavailable — try 10:30 or Room B".
      WHO CALLS: YOUR tool/server writes it; the MODEL consumes it.
    end note

    :T7 · Decide RETRY vs not;
    note right
      RETRY only TRANSIENT faults (timeout, rate limit)
      => back off + retry.
      DON'T retry deterministic faults (bad ID) — wasted tokens.
      PARTIAL SUCCESS: asked 50, got 30 ->
      return the 30 + say which 20 failed & why.
      TRAP: silently returning partial data as if complete.
      ----
      WHY USED : not every failure is worth trying again.
      SOLVES   : wasted tokens/latency on doomed calls; infinite loops.
      EXAMPLE  : timeout=TRANSIENT->retry+backoff; bad date=CORRECT
               & re-call; room taken=CHANGE approach (new room/time).
      WHO CALLS: hard CAP = APP code (deterministic); soft stop =
               MODEL judgement. Enforce in code, guide via prompt.
    end note
  else (NO)
    #LightGreen:Result returned to Claude;
  endif
}

' ---------- TOPICS 8–11: SCOPING & CONTROL ----------
partition "T8–T11 · SCOPING & CONTROL (design-time)" {
  :T8 · SCOPE tools PER AGENT (least privilege);
  note right
    Every tool def sits in context EVERY turn.
    Give each agent ONLY the tools its job needs.
    Fewer tools => cheaper + sharper routing
    (fewer look-alikes to confuse).
    Research subagent gets search+read, NOT delete/refund.
    ----
    WHY USED : capability is GRANTED, never inherited from parent.
    SOLVES   : (1) cost+routing noise (2) BLAST RADIUS — an agent
               CANNOT do what it was never given (injection/error safe).
    EXAMPLE  : product-Q&A subagent has NO refund tool, so a poisoned
               doc can't trigger a refund.
    WHO CALLS: YOU (architect) assign per agent at design time.
  end note

  :T9 · SELECTION HEURISTICS & FAILURES;
  note right
    Heuristics: closest semantic match wins;
    specific beats general.
    3 FAILURES (all from fuzzy boundaries):
    * OVERLAPPING — 2 tools mean the same thing
    * HALLUCINATED — nothing fits, Claude invents a call
    * OVER-CALLING — calls tools nobody asked for
    Fix for all three: sharper description boundaries.
    ----
    WHY USED : name/predict the ways selection goes wrong.
    SOLVES   : diagnosing WHY a wrong tool fired.
    EXAMPLE  : two "search" tools overlap -> misroute; fix = add
               "NOT for..." scoping to each.
    WHO CALLS: MODEL exhibits the failure; YOU fix the descriptions.
    NOTE     : Claude can't fabricate a NEW tool name — it forces the
               CLOSEST existing tool. That's the real hazard.
  end note

  :T10 · CONTROL TOOL SELECTION (tool_choice);
  note right
    * auto  — Claude decides (default)
    * any   — must call SOME tool (Claude picks which)
    * named — must call THIS specific tool
    * none  — call nothing
    Use "named" for pipelines where every request
    must hit the same tool (e.g. extraction).
    ----
    WHY USED : sometimes you must OVERRIDE the model's discretion.
    SOLVES   : non-determinism where a step must ALWAYS call a tool.
    EXAMPLE  : extraction pipeline pins tool_choice=named(extract).
    WHO CALLS: YOU set it in the API request; it binds the MODEL.
  end note

  :T11 · TOOL SEARCH for LARGE catalogs;
  note right
    Can't load 200 tool defs — context + accuracy collapse.
    Load ONE search tool instead:
    Claude describes need -> search returns the few
    relevant defs -> those enter context -> Claude calls.
    "Know the catalog," don't memorize the library.
    (Two-step: FIND the tool, then USE the tool.)
    ----
    WHY USED : huge catalogs blow the context + wreck routing.
    SOLVES   : loading 200 defs at once (cost + accuracy collapse).
    EXAMPLE  : Claude needs "refund" -> search returns 3 defs ->
               those enter context -> Claude calls the right one.
    WHO CALLS: MODEL calls the search tool, then the found tool;
               YOU provide the single search entry point.
  end note
}

#LightGreen:Correct tool · valid inputs · scoped · controlled;
stop

floating note left
  == PART A EXAM TRAPS (memorize cold) ==
  1. PRIMARY ROUTING SIGNAL = the DESCRIPTION.
     Distractors: name / schema / output type / signature — ALL wrong.
     A perfect name + clean schema STILL misroutes if the desc is vague.
  2. Classic collision: a tool named "search" (web? db? file? vector?)
     — only the DESCRIPTION disambiguates.
  3. STRICT MODE constrains generation; it does NOT reject afterward.
  4. FALLBACK is a GOOD failure (ask) vs MISROUTE (confident wrong).
  5. More tools = more cost AND more misrouting => least privilege.
  ----
  Flow in one line: DESCRIPTION routes -> SCHEMA/STRICT validate ->
  ERRORS/RETRY recover -> SCOPE + tool_choice + tool_search control.
end note

@enduml
